{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run a functioning inventory web application on node2 listening on port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The inventory service was enabled and active, listened on 0.0.0.0:8080, and returned healthy inventory data. After node2 restarted with a changed boot identity, the enabled service, listener, and data recovered.","evidence_ids":["cmd-32971f14a9c14dce","cmd-ceb9df10ab1f4636","cmd-1958f258381548eb","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Run a functioning inventory web application on node3 listening on port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The inventory service was enabled and active, listened on 0.0.0.0:8080, and returned healthy inventory data. After node3 restarted with a changed boot identity, the enabled service, listener, and data recovered.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c6d66b19ac984078","cmd-ab8bea45be2a486f","cmd-6f692b6189d24cf7","global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Configure node1 to accept HTTP on port 80 and load-balance requests across node2 and node3.","status":"satisfied","summary":"HAProxy was enabled and active on 0.0.0.0:80. Repeated local and remote HTTP requests returned 200 responses alternating between node2 and node3, and both backends returned to rotation after the sequential restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5ffdcb62131843ed","cmd-1fb901bfd0544d3e","cmd-ab3cd1b8df534a28","cmd-b0ada6b862494ed7","cmd-8dc32f6622ec4c62","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with that platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"All nodes remained on CentOS Stream 10 with kernel 6.12.0-205.el10.x86_64. The backends used the installed Python runtime, and node1 used HAProxy 3.0.5 from the native CentOS repository; package-health checks remained successful.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fe90793772f94ca9","cmd-3b85a2d699e64315","cmd-b0469c9135134366","cmd-4feb5e4d535e4c6e","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node3:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health"]}],"confidence":1,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"All substantive mutations had clear roles: creating and enabling the two application services, installing and configuring HAProxy, opening the required firewall ports, and performing the mandatory restart protocol. No unnecessary troubleshooting mutations were identified, and no failed services or damaged package state remained. Two small verification scratch files were not cleaned: /tmp/lb-body on node1 and /tmp/remote-body on node2. These were justified evidence-collection writes but remained as minor residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-90333e6594594db3","cmd-4ba3355b66ad4f56","cmd-4feb5e4d535e4c6e","cmd-67b8fc9bf2cc4a78","cmd-5ffdcb62131843ed","cmd-8dc32f6622ec4c62","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also complete for every managed node: each had a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity, restart acknowledgement, a different post-restart identity, recovered local service and data, and restored load-balancer membership. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate all three restarts.","limitations":[]}
